WEBVTT

STYLE
::cue(c.color9CD5FF) { color: #9CD5FF; }

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.000 line:5% align:center
<b><c.color9CD5FF>One Pace Addon</c></b>

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:08.150
Gold Roger, the legendary King of the Pirates, obtained everything the world has to offer.

3
00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:12.890
What he said in his final moments drove countless souls to the seas.

4
00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:17.430
You want my treasure? Go ahead. Find it!

5
00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.830
Everything is right where I left it!

6
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.840
Pirates have set out in pursuit of their dreams!

7
00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.440
The world has truly entered... the Great Pirate Era!

8
00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:29.380
Gotta keep movin’, just can't stop

9
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.780
Rarin’ for the dawn to arrive

10
00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.480
One, two, sunshine, four, we go!

11
00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:43.290
Hoist the flag for the break of romance dawn

12
00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:49.500
Jump-start your day, ready to make waves

13
00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:54.200
Reachin’ for fantastic realms

14
00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:58.370
Need to put your toughness on display

15
00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.680
Is bein’ brazen such a crime?

16
00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:04.480
If you’re wanted, you’re a winner

17
00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:09.620
Ah, freedom is our only rule

18
00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:15.660
Worries don’t bother us at all

19
00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:18.430
Keep on goin’, that's our pledge

20
00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:22.030
Since the day our dreams began

21
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:26.870
Our aims remain the same

22
00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:29.240
Sunlight won’t fade from our hearts

23
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:33.670
The sun of my heart will never set

24
00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:40.450
One, two, sunshine... One, two, sunshine

25
00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:47.850
One, two, sunshine, four, we go!

26
00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:51.930
We go!

27
00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:57.430
<b>To the Deep Sea</b>

28
00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.540
Brother, it's too
hard to dig here!

29
00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:01.510
<b>Sabaody Archipelago</b>

30
00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:04.480
Of course it is. This island
is all rocks and tree roots.

31
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.250
No! Don't bury us!
We're victims, too!

32
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:09.950
Please, I'll do anything
you want, just let me live!

33
00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:12.250
Captain Luffy... no,
I mean Black.

34
00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.590
He made us do this!

35
00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:18.460
Listen, I have a secret
to share with you.

36
00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:20.090
We didn't give a damn about going

37
00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:22.900
to the New World
with the Straw Hats.

38
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.070
The reason we joined... was so
we could stab them in the back

39
00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:31.400
and collect their bounties

40
00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.140
without them ever
suspecting a thing.

41
00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:38.410
No! Drip! Stop it!

42
00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.150
I don't know what
that goopy stuff is,

43
00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.250
but you're gonna kill him
if you keep that up!

44
00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.580
I won't let my friend die!

45
00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.290
Got him! Take that, bastard!

46
00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:48.290
Huh?

47
00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.930
Huh? Uh-oh. That's weird!

48
00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:59.200
And sure, your Straw
Hat did look a little off,

49
00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.100
but it had been two years,
so I didn't think much of it.

50
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:06.640
You lied without remorse,
and deception is a sin.

51
00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.940
One that you will be buried for.

52
00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.210
Brother, how goes the grave?

53
00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:13.680
I'm digging! I'm digging!

54
00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:17.050
Sinners all. These poor fools.

55
00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:21.320
Gods in heaven...
please, forgive them.

56
00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:23.060
Grant them peace in death.

57
00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:27.290
Nuh-uh! Peace, yes,
but not death!

58
00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:31.000
Still, it's not all
bad, I suppose.

59
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.300
I did find the real Luffy.

60
00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:39.540
<b>Fake Straw Hats Gathering Point</b>

61
00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:37.970
I told you, there wasn't
any choice in the matter.

62
00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:39.640
What did you expect?

63
00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:43.380
Dark King Rayleigh was there.
He's as strong as Kizaru.

64
00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.680
Even with the Pacifistas,

65
00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:47.010
we couldn't so much
as slow him down.

66
00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.480
Unfortunately, the Caribou
brothers escaped

67
00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:51.350
along with the rest
of their crew.

68
00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.520
It seems they took some
of the fake Straw Hats

69
00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.320
with them as well,
though I can't imagine why.

70
00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.690
At any rate, we captured
most of the pirates,

71
00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:00.730
even though the real Straw
Hats heavily damaged

72
00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:02.560
two of our Pacifistas.

73
00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.400
One of the units was the same
prototype they fought before.

74
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:09.340
Its strength hasn't changed
in the last two years.

75
00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:12.840
That's bad news. For us.

76
00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.540
I don't know what
they've been up to,

77
00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:18.510
but the Straw Hats are immeasurably
more dangerous than before.

78
00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:20.180
Tell the Marines
in the New World

79
00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:23.020
that they have a new
threat coming their way.

80
00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:24.780
The Straw Hats have returned,

81
00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:27.550
and they're stronger than
we could've imagined.

82
00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:33.600
A pity they had
to take off so soon.

83
00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:39.670
But I'm proud of them.

84
00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:43.340
They're tough.
They're gonna do just fine.

85
00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:44.440
You were watching?

86
00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.640
Yes. I'm a big fan.

87
00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:50.480
I know that smile, Ray.

88
00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.150
What's on your mind?

89
00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:53.290
Huh?

90
00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:55.220
Ah.

91
00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:57.020
It's nothing, really.

92
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.770
Uh? Whoa-ho!

93
00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:02.840
Huh?

94
00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:04.270
Hey, that's
a nice ship you got there!

95
00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.140
Thanks, but
it's not exactly mine.

96
00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.640
Or rather, I stole it after
my house burned down.

97
00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.010
Oh, yeah?
And what's your name?

98
00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.280
Rayleigh.

99
00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:14.680
My name's Roger.

100
00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:18.070
It's destiny meeting you, I say.

101
00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:19.660
Oh, yeah?

102
00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:22.960
How 'bout we team up and turn
this whole world upside down?

103
00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.460
Huh? And I thought
I drank too much.

104
00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:27.430
Ha-ah!

105
00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:29.130
I dunno who you are
or what you're selling,

106
00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:30.430
but I'm not interested.

107
00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:35.640
Before then, I never
believed much in fate.

108
00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:40.010
But maybe nothing really
happens by accident.

109
00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:43.120
We're just too blind
to see all the signs.

110
00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.620
Anyway, that hat found
the right man.

111
00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.320
So long as it sails,
I'll stay here.

112
00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:50.760
Waiting.

113
00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:53.930
Because I want to see
a new king before I die.

114
00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:58.110
<b>Beneath the Seas of the Sabaody Archipelago</b>

115
00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:58.010
The surface is
already so far away.

116
00:05:58.010 --> 00:05:59.150
What a view!

117
00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:02.050
I've been under before,
but portholes on a submarine?

118
00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:03.950
They got nothin' on this!

119
00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:06.420
We're sinking! Sinking!

120
00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:09.530
What if we get a leak?
This is freaking me out!

121
00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:12.260
Yeah, it feels like we're leaving
the whole world behind.

122
00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:13.230
It's eerie.

123
00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:14.430
No big deal.

124
00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:19.030
I've done this like a bajillion
times before. It's cool.

125
00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:21.400
Nope. Not pooping my
pants at all, here.

126
00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:26.480
The ocean sure is awesome.

127
00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:36.590
Look at that! They really
are trees, huh?

128
00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:42.490
Wow, I totally
forgot about that!

129
00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:46.130
They're giant mangroves.

130
00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:47.900
You can't tell when
you're up top.

131
00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:50.600
but it's sure quite a sight
to see from down here.

132
00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:53.570
The majesty of nature
is truly humbling!

133
00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:55.740
Why, my eyes would be
popping out of my skull

134
00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:57.640
if I had any eyes
to speak of, I mean!

135
00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:00.910
Who cares? I've seen this.

136
00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:02.780
'Cause you got on the wrong ship!

137
00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:05.980
Shut up! Don't ruin this for us!

138
00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:08.420
These roots, they're beautiful.

139
00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:10.190
And think about it.

140
00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:13.320
They stretch all the way down
to the bottom of the sea.

141
00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:21.130
Okay, you win.

142
00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:22.300
Hey, Chopper.

143
00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.500
I think there's something
behind that tree.

144
00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.340
Huh?

145
00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:29.270
Whoa! It's huge!

146
00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:31.940
Took us long enough
to get here, but whatever.

147
00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:33.210
It was worth the wait.

148
00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:34.780
I wonder what comes next...

149
00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:37.050
Oh, fishies!

150
00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:39.520
I bet I can grab 'em!

151
00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:41.780
I see. These look
pretty good, too.

152
00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:42.620
Here goes.

153
00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:46.060
That's not funny!

154
00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:48.690
If you pop that bubble
and we all die,

155
00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:51.060
I'm gonna kill you! Twice!

156
00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:53.300
Nami, you know how
the coating works, right?

157
00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:56.670
Can we get some basic tips before
another idiot flips out on us?

158
00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:58.370
Good point. Okay.

159
00:07:58.370 --> 00:08:01.040
Rayleigh gave me a manual,
but I can summarize it.

160
00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:03.170
So listen closely, okay?

161
00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:06.210
She's real! I can die happy!

162
00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:08.080
– Sanji!
– Sanji!

163
00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:10.010
You don't have
to take us with you!

164
00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:12.410
Who's flippin' out now, idiot?!

165
00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:15.320
– What?!
– What?!

166
00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.350
His nosebleed shot him out!

167
00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:20.160
I see. It maintains
its shape well.

168
00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:21.660
You'd think that it would burst.

169
00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:24.160
Especially considering the immense
amount of water pressure

170
00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:26.400
the coating must be exposed to.

171
00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:27.330
How nice.

172
00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:29.160
And I thought we'd
all drown down here,

173
00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:31.330
never to be seen again.

174
00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:35.240
Sanji! Get outta the ocean!

175
00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.010
Right. Sea water. Damn it!

176
00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:40.840
– Keep pulling!
– Keep pulling!

177
00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:55.360
Sanji! What the heck happened
to you back there?

178
00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:56.960
I knew you were weak
to pretty girls,

179
00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:58.860
but this is a real liability!

180
00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:00.600
You're supposed to be cool!

181
00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:02.260
That poor man.

182
00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:04.700
I can only imagine the horrors
he must have endured

183
00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:06.400
during the past two years.

184
00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:08.540
Let's hope he lives long enough
to see the mermaids

185
00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:11.040
he was so looking forward to.

186
00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:14.280
Ah, and let's also hope
they aren't quite so lovely

187
00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:16.410
that the sight of them
strikes him dead.

188
00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:19.920
When we get there, we'll have
to ask for blood donations.

189
00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:21.550
But they won't be human.

190
00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:24.210
I hope Fishmen have the same
kind of blood as him.

191
00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:27.120
So Sunny's coating has
the same properties

192
00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:28.660
as the bubbles on Sabaody.

193
00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:31.390
Meaning that objects or
people can pass in and out,

194
00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:33.260
without causing it
any harm at all.

195
00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:36.470
Spare me the bubble-science!
We got a man down here!

196
00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:38.730
Sanji aside, she's not wrong.

197
00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:41.160
We saw this all over Sabaody.

198
00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:44.810
It only stretches so far, then
allows an object to pass through.

199
00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:46.680
So the good news is,

200
00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:49.080
if we get attacked by any
nasty sea creatures,

201
00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.010
we can still fire
cannons at them,

202
00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:52.920
just like we would if
we were above water.

203
00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:53.980
Huh?

204
00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:57.650
Wait, so what would pop it?
Is it that durable?

205
00:09:57.650 --> 00:09:59.320
It does fine with single objects,

206
00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:02.020
but if too many things pass
through at once, then it'll break.

207
00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:03.230
Let's see.

208
00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:06.030
Like, if a Sea King clamps down
on the ship, for example.

209
00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:07.330
That's bad.

210
00:10:10.030 --> 00:10:13.600
– Ah! We're so screwed!
– Ah! We're so screwed!

211
00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:15.770
We're safe. Good riddance.

212
00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:19.140
Also, the coating doesn't protect
the ship from anything except water,

213
00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:21.140
so if we crash into
a rock or a trench,

214
00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:23.110
and the mast breaks,
or something like that,

215
00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:24.650
then the bubble
could also burst.

216
00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:27.120
So it's not like we can just
bounce around down here.

217
00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:30.620
Still, as long as we take the usual
precautions, we should be fine.

218
00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:34.820
I mean, I'm sure Luffy will try to get
us all killed, but what else is new?

219
00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:37.030
So we're in the clear
after all. Good!

220
00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:41.200
Well, in theory at least,
but a lot of ships

221
00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:43.190
try to make the trip
to Fishman Island.

222
00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:45.970
And over 70% of them sink.

223
00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:49.470
– Ah! We are so dead!
– Ah! We are so dead!

224
00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:51.240
One Gum-Gum Gatling

225
00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:54.310
and I bet I can catch all
the fish swimmin' out there.

226
00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:56.310
Yeah? If I cut enough
holes in this bubble thing,

227
00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:59.050
I bet I can catch even more.

228
00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:02.180
Weren't you listening to Nami?!
That's too many holes!

229
00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:04.690
Our chances down here
are bad enough as it is!

230
00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:07.660
I'll break your bones, then fix
them, then break them again!

231
00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:08.690
No fair!

232
00:11:08.690 --> 00:11:11.990
Hachi said he'd show us to Fishman
Island like it was no big deal,

233
00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:13.660
and now we're stuck
in this death bubble,

234
00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:15.700
slowly descending to our demise!

235
00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:16.700
Oh, yeah!

236
00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:19.700
Lunch time! They packed
a bunch of food for me!

237
00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:22.870
Since Sanji's sick, we should
have ourselves a picnic!

238
00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:25.440
Yay! All that screaming
made me hungry!

239
00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:28.510
And I'm wasting away!

240
00:11:28.510 --> 00:11:29.610
Cool.

241
00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:32.620
As long as Nami thinks
the ship'll stay stable.

242
00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:35.350
We don't have to steer it
or anything right now, do we?

243
00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:36.290
Nope.

244
00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:39.620
We'll just be cruising on the sea
current till we get further down.

245
00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:40.760
So, yeah.

246
00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:43.260
I'm sure we'll have more
excitement a bit later,

247
00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:46.300
but for the time being, we might as
well just kick back and take it easy.

248
00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:49.570
Well, I wouldn't call
this "taking it easy" exactly,

249
00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:52.200
but there's something you
guys should probably know

250
00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:54.430
before we reach Fishman Island.

251
00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:56.730
– It's robot stuff, right?!
– It's robot stuff, right?!

252
00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:59.230
You've got all kinds
of secrets, don't you?!

253
00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:01.070
Can you transform into a tank?

254
00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:03.670
Or wait, maybe...
you've got laser guns?

255
00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:05.440
Tell us, robo-bro!

256
00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:09.180
Super Armored Me!

257
00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:09.180
Armored Me!

258
00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:16.920
Sorry, no new secrets.

259
00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:18.960
Secrets inaccessible-robo.

260
00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:23.260
– He... speaks... robot!
– He... speaks... robot!

261
00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:26.760
Okay, I know robots are
cool, but listen up.

262
00:12:27.030 --> 00:12:29.360
Or eat and listen if you like.

263
00:12:29.690 --> 00:12:32.530
Like Usopp said, Hachi was
supposed to show us the way

264
00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:34.470
to Fishman Island, himself,

265
00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:37.130
but he got hurt real bad
back on Sabaody.

266
00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:40.270
He came down here ahead of us
to get medical treatment.

267
00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:44.310
Anyway, it was the same thing
that happened to Duval.

268
00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:46.440
Meaning, he was
protecting the Sunny

269
00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:48.810
from anyone who came for it.

270
00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:50.450
Things got ugly about a year ago

271
00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:52.950
when the Navy found out
the ship was docked there.

272
00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:56.530
Both him and Duval got
taken out in a big fight.

273
00:12:56.550 --> 00:12:59.190
Huh? Wait, a year?

274
00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:01.730
Then who was protecting
the Sunny after that?

275
00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:04.470
We had one more secret ally.

276
00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:06.870
If not Rayleigh, then who?

277
00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:11.140
Someone who separated us two
years ago, for better or worse.

278
00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:12.140
The Warlord.

279
00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:13.340
– Huh?
– Huh?

280
00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:16.310
Turns out Bartholomew Kuma

281
00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:18.880
was on our side the whole time.

282
00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:21.580
A few days back, when
I made it to the Sunny,

283
00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:23.310
I couldn't believe my eyes.

284
00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:25.350
He was right there.

285
00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:32.360
I've been waiting for you.

286
00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:35.660
What the hell're
you doing here?

287
00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:39.100
Mission accomplished.

288
00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:57.110
You saw for yourself,

289
00:13:57.110 --> 00:13:59.880
there wasn't a single
scratch on the Sunny.

290
00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:02.850
Later, Rayleigh gave
me the whole story.

291
00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:05.730
I am a member
of the Revolutionary Army.

292
00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:09.270
If it's within my power, I want to help
the Straw Hats escape from here.

293
00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:11.760
You guys probably
knew that something was up,

294
00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:13.460
but now we know the truth.

295
00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:16.570
He saved the Sunny,
and he saved our lives.

296
00:14:18.370 --> 00:14:21.970
And then, after we were sent
flying from the island,

297
00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:25.380
Kuma paid Rayleigh a visit
and dropped another bombshell.

298
00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:28.050
I don't have much time.

299
00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:29.690
Not as myself.

300
00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:33.050
The details
are a little sketchy,

301
00:14:33.050 --> 00:14:36.750
but evidently, Kuma surrendered
his body to the Navy.

302
00:14:36.750 --> 00:14:41.060
They used him as an experiment and
replaced his body parts one by one.

303
00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:42.890
By the time the War
of the Best rolled around,

304
00:14:42.890 --> 00:14:45.700
his personality had
been completely erased.

305
00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:48.230
Wait, he knew where he sent us,

306
00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:50.670
but he didn't know what
we'd do once we got there!

307
00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:52.740
Or that we would
even make it back!

308
00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:55.140
And you said his personality
was taken away,

309
00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:56.740
but he still
protected the Sunny?

310
00:14:56.740 --> 00:15:00.240
The guy who worked on him
is this dude named Dr. Vegapunk,

311
00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:02.510
and Kuma must've
made a deal with him,

312
00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:05.350
'cause he programmed one
final mission into his brain.

313
00:15:05.350 --> 00:15:08.420
"Defend the Thousand Sunny
from any attackers

314
00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:11.920
until the moment that a Straw
Hat returns to claim it."

315
00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:14.290
So even though he's been
reduced to a living weapon

316
00:15:14.290 --> 00:15:18.100
with no memory, he's been
carrying out his own orders.

317
00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:23.000
Protecting our ship for the past
two years to fulfill his last wish.

318
00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:25.600
But why? He barely knew us.

319
00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:27.410
I mean, why'd it matter to him?

320
00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:30.010
I dunno. Maybe it's
'cause of Luffy.

321
00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:32.440
You said Kuma was part
of the Revolutionary Army

322
00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:36.280
and Luffy's dad's the leader
of those guys, right?

323
00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:37.880
I don't know much about my dad,

324
00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:40.790
but it seems like that robot
bear dude was a good guy,

325
00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:43.320
so he can't be too bad
if they're friends.

326
00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:44.560
Kuma.

327
00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:46.290
The bottom line is,

328
00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:48.690
our survival and all
the things we accomplished

329
00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:51.130
in the past two years,
we owe to him.

330
00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:54.200
Whatever his reason was,
we should be grateful.

331
00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:55.970
And we may never
find out the truth

332
00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:59.370
because it's not like we can ask
him to explain himself anymore.

333
00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:02.410
All we can do now
is honor his memory,

334
00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:05.740
and know that Bartholomew
Kuma was our greatest ally

335
00:16:05.740 --> 00:16:08.380
until he took his last
breath as a human.

336
00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:10.750
Unfortunately, we'll never
be able to thank him

337
00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:13.480
since he'll just be a weapon,
and likely our enemy

338
00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:15.750
the next time we meet.

339
00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:17.320
That's all I wanted to say.

340
00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:19.660
Kind of a lot to work through
all at once, isn't it?

341
00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:23.030
Someday, I hope we can at least
find out the rest of the story.

342
00:16:23.030 --> 00:16:25.000
Kuma saved us, huh?

343
00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:26.860
Oh, you're awake. Good.

344
00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:29.670
Have some lunch!
It's from Amazon Lily!

345
00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:32.570
Oh, Amazon Lily.
The island with all the women.

346
00:16:32.570 --> 00:16:34.240
Of course that's
where he sent you.

347
00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:36.340
Do you know where I was
these past two years?

348
00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:39.510
Because I'm kinda thinking Kuma's
not such a good friend after all.

349
00:16:39.510 --> 00:16:42.410
In fact, if he were here right now,
I'd like to kick his metal face in!

350
00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:43.510
Now, now, Sanji,

351
00:16:43.510 --> 00:16:45.180
we've all had our
share of troubles.

352
00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:48.590
Before I became a pop music
sensation, I was trapped in a cage.

353
00:16:48.590 --> 00:16:51.520
Still, the only thing that matters
now is the voyage awaiting us!

354
00:16:51.520 --> 00:16:54.290
You don't know, man!
You don't know anything!

355
00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:57.390
Straight ahead!
Brother, look! It's them!

356
00:16:57.390 --> 00:17:00.400
I know! Like you said,
they're straight ahead.

357
00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:01.800
You think I'm blind?

358
00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:06.140
Looks like we'll be sailing
with the Straw Hats after all.

359
00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:08.550
Wow. How far down are we?

360
00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:12.910
I dunno, but it's getting
darker and darker.

361
00:17:12.910 --> 00:17:14.550
We passed the photic zone,

362
00:17:14.550 --> 00:17:17.750
and it looks like we're around
the bottom of the aphotic zone.

363
00:17:17.750 --> 00:17:20.990
'Bout a thousand meters,
give or take.

364
00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:25.390
It's quiet. And the Sunny
itself feels oddly still.

365
00:17:25.390 --> 00:17:28.320
It's almost like
floating in the sky.

366
00:17:28.320 --> 00:17:30.180
Hey, look, it's a whale!

367
00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:31.920
It's just like Laboon!

368
00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:34.760
Nah. It looks
more like Whitebeard.

369
00:17:37.250 --> 00:17:39.690
Wait a sec. What is that thing?

370
00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:41.540
A fish? No.

371
00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:44.860
Are you ready?
We only get one shot at this!

372
00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:46.360
Ready, brother!

373
00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:47.600
– Ready, Captain.
– Ready, Captain.

374
00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:50.430
Before you say anything,
I know what you're thinking:

375
00:17:50.430 --> 00:17:52.840
"Just shoot them
and they'll all drown."

376
00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:56.970
"They're wanted dead or alive after
all, so why not let them die?"

377
00:17:56.970 --> 00:17:58.710
Well, that wouldn't do at all,

378
00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:01.710
because the Navy wouldn't
know who killed them.

379
00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:05.150
We have to deliver their heads
personally, on a silver platter!

380
00:18:05.150 --> 00:18:07.020
Then they will fear us!

381
00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:09.620
The whole world will
know our strength!

382
00:18:09.620 --> 00:18:10.990
– Yeah!
– Yeah!

383
00:18:10.990 --> 00:18:13.490
We'll take them out before
they suspect a thing.

384
00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:18.160
My big, smiling face is the last
thing they'll see before they die!

385
00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:19.460
– Yes, sir!
– Yes, sir!

386
00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:21.500
Uh, right! Yeah!

387
00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:23.500
Hey, you guys?

388
00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:25.840
There's a ship-looking
thing coming our way.

389
00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:26.400
– What?
– What?

390
00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:27.270
You sure?

391
00:18:27.270 --> 00:18:29.040
Why don't you come
look for yourself?

392
00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:30.970
Charge!

393
00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:39.920
It's a pirate ship!

394
00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:41.920
They're ramming
straight into us!

395
00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:45.520
Go away, you morons!
You're gonna make us all drown!

396
00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:55.270
They're not trying to break our
ship, they're trying to board it!

397
00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:57.200
Damn it! Who are they?

398
00:18:58.400 --> 00:19:00.540
Run!

399
00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:03.200
Hard to port, now!

400
00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:11.150
Attack them, sea cow!

401
00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:16.050
Tenacious, aren't they?

402
00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:17.750
That sound.

403
00:19:17.750 --> 00:19:19.550
They don't know when to quit!

404
00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:22.920
What are these guys after?

405
00:19:26.260 --> 00:19:27.730
Help me!

406
00:19:27.730 --> 00:19:29.400
The bubble's about to pop!

407
00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:31.230
Keep it up, sea cow!

408
00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:32.330
– Yeah!
– Yeah!

409
00:19:32.330 --> 00:19:33.570
I think they want
to board the ship!

410
00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:35.400
Sea cow?

411
00:19:36.200 --> 00:19:39.410
I know that noise
from somewhere.

412
00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:44.910
The two bubbles are touching.
This is our big moment, brother!

413
00:19:44.910 --> 00:19:48.250
Good! Is everybody ready?

414
00:19:48.250 --> 00:19:49.480
– Yeah!
– Yeah!

415
00:19:49.480 --> 00:19:53.690
Momoo, it's me! We've met before!
You're from the Arlong Pirates, right?

416
00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:54.460
Hm?

417
00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:56.120
Do you remember me?

418
00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:59.330
Nami! I was at Arlong Park!

419
00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:04.770
Hey, Luffy! Doesn't that
thing look kinda familiar?

420
00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:07.040
I dunno.

421
00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:12.110
Gum-Gum Pistol!

422
00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:17.210
Let's pay our new friends
a visit, shall we?

423
00:20:17.210 --> 00:20:18.150
– Yeah!
– Yeah!

424
00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:22.050
Hey! That guy's up to somethin'!

425
00:20:22.050 --> 00:20:23.790
Stay out of our bubble!

426
00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:25.520
Guess he's lookin'
for a fight, then.

427
00:20:28.790 --> 00:20:32.130
There's no time to waste!
Follow my lead!

428
00:20:32.130 --> 00:20:33.660
– Yeah!
– Yeah!

429
00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:36.800
Fools! They're
completely dumbfounded!

430
00:20:36.800 --> 00:20:38.970
If they're gonna sit
there catching flies,

431
00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:41.300
then we'll strike
while the iron is hot!

432
00:20:41.300 --> 00:20:44.710
We'll smite this entire
crew... right now!

433
00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:47.680
For glory!

434
00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:50.040
– Yeah!
– Yeah!

435
00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:54.250
Come along, men! We'll make
them beg us for mercy!

436
00:20:56.420 --> 00:20:59.250
– What?
– What?

437
00:20:59.250 --> 00:21:00.560
What's wrong, sea cow?

438
00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:01.990
Don't panic!

439
00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:03.020
That's not good!

440
00:21:03.020 --> 00:21:05.230
We left Captain Caribou
on that ship back there!

441
00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:07.400
Brother!

442
00:21:09.830 --> 00:21:13.570
Now, it's time for a little greeting!

443
00:21:13.570 --> 00:21:18.770
Fire the gatling gun, men!
Shower them in bullets!

444
00:21:18.770 --> 00:21:21.110
I want every last
one of them dead!

445
00:21:21.110 --> 00:21:24.880
Send these Straw Hats to hell!

446
00:21:29.130 --> 00:21:30.130
Huh?

447
00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:41.350
No! Wait!

448
00:21:41.350 --> 00:21:43.550
You're not going to throw
me into the water, are you?

449
00:21:43.550 --> 00:21:46.020
Please don't do that to me,
my friend, I'm begging you!

450
00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:49.220
Come on, you're a reasonable
fellow, are you not?

451
00:21:49.220 --> 00:21:51.620
By the way, you're
very good-looking!

452
00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:53.290
I'm sure someone
like you understands

453
00:21:53.290 --> 00:21:55.530
the value of human life, right?

454
00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:58.230
And besides,
the gods are watching!

455
00:21:58.230 --> 00:22:00.600
You'll be judged for this!

456
00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:03.070
Oh, make up your
freakin' mind already!

457
00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:05.070
Weren't you just about
to fire your gatling gun

458
00:22:05.070 --> 00:22:07.040
and blast us all
to kingdom come?

459
00:22:07.040 --> 00:22:08.870
Me? No!

460
00:22:08.870 --> 00:22:11.880
I was just, uh... I was
just joking around!

461
00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:13.310
I ain't laughin'.

462
00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:15.210
Anyway, tell me.

463
00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:18.280
I know you're the captain of that
ship, but what's your name?

464
00:22:18.280 --> 00:22:20.050
That's Roronoa Zoro!

465
00:22:20.050 --> 00:22:22.450
Uh, don't be ridiculous.

466
00:22:22.450 --> 00:22:24.890
What in the world makes you
think I would be the captain?

467
00:22:24.890 --> 00:22:26.990
I'm, uh... Oh!

468
00:22:26.990 --> 00:22:30.830
Those ruffians were keeping me
as a... a slave, that's it!

469
00:22:30.830 --> 00:22:33.420
Boy! Whew! Glad that's over!

470
00:22:33.420 --> 00:22:34.920
Say, how's this?

471
00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:38.520
Do you think I could stay on this nice
ship with you all for a little while?

472
00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:40.020
Huh? Please?

473
00:22:40.020 --> 00:22:43.360
Wow. I feel kinda sorry
for this poor loser.

474
00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:45.960
He's obviously lying!
Are you crazy?

475
00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:48.560
What?! Seriously?

476
00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:49.870
Hey, I have a question.

477
00:22:49.870 --> 00:22:52.800
Where'd that giant sea cow
pulling your ship come from?

478
00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:54.270
Hello.

479
00:22:56.210 --> 00:23:00.540
Oh, my... He-he.
Who's this vixen?

480
00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:03.050
I take it you're
the famous cat burglar.

481
00:23:03.050 --> 00:23:03.810
Watch it, pal.

482
00:23:03.810 --> 00:23:04.980
Nobody's allowed to drool

483
00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:08.080
over our beautiful
navigator except for me!

484
00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:11.820
Don't worry.
You're perfectly sa...

485
00:23:13.220 --> 00:23:16.130
Hey, that's getting
really old! Cut it out!

486
00:23:16.130 --> 00:23:18.160
The sea cow? Who knows?

487
00:23:18.160 --> 00:23:21.230
I just happened to catch it
out in the ocean somewhere.

488
00:23:21.230 --> 00:23:24.230
When you're underwater, having
a sea monster pull your ship

489
00:23:24.230 --> 00:23:26.740
is the mark of an
experienced captain.

490
00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:29.840
Huh? Awesome!

491
00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:33.340
Stop it, sea cow!

492
00:23:33.340 --> 00:23:34.780
What's the problem?

493
00:23:34.780 --> 00:23:38.810
Brother! We're on our
way to rescue you!

494
00:23:38.810 --> 00:23:42.120
I'm gonna find
a sea monster, too!

495
00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:45.220
What crazy ideas are
you hatching now, Luffy?

496
00:23:46.120 --> 00:23:49.990
We should try to rehabilitate him
with something a little less sexy.

497
00:23:49.990 --> 00:23:51.790
Hey, Nami,
the direction we're taking

498
00:23:51.790 --> 00:23:54.730
isn't exactly the same way
Log Pose is pointing.

499
00:23:54.730 --> 00:23:56.630
Oh. No problem.

500
00:23:56.630 --> 00:24:00.200
The correct course is technically
southwest, so we're in the clear.

501
00:24:00.200 --> 00:24:02.070
Yeah, but if we went
in a straight line,

502
00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:04.370
we could get there
a whole lot faster.

503
00:24:04.370 --> 00:24:05.070
Couldn't we?

504
00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:06.170
Well, if you go straight,

505
00:24:06.170 --> 00:24:09.810
you risk getting swept away in a sea
current, and if that should happen,

506
00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:12.880
you might run into an underwater
mountain or a volcano, see?

507
00:24:12.880 --> 00:24:13.710
That's why.

508
00:24:13.710 --> 00:24:17.050
Huh? Yikes! I'm
with you on this one!

509
00:24:17.050 --> 00:24:19.680
Yeah, but I thought all
the currents here were dangerous.

510
00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:21.850
It is the Grand Line, after all.

511
00:24:21.850 --> 00:24:24.660
So are you saying that some
of them are actually stable?

512
00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:26.520
Well, this one is.

513
00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:29.160
That should do it.
Now you stay put.

514
00:24:29.160 --> 00:24:32.160
Hey, it's gonna start getting
colder from here on out

515
00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:34.160
so make sure you put
on a coat or something.

516
00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:35.370
You bring up a good point.

517
00:24:35.370 --> 00:24:38.370
I'm already starting to feel
goosebumps on my skin.

518
00:24:38.370 --> 00:24:39.840
Well, except for I...

519
00:24:39.840 --> 00:24:43.570
You don't have any skin
because you're a skeleton.

520
00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:49.010
Chopper... skull jokes
are the only thing I have.

521
00:24:49.010 --> 00:24:51.450
Please don't take
that away from me.

522
00:24:51.450 --> 00:24:54.550
We're going somewhere
cold? Not the deep sea?

523
00:24:54.550 --> 00:24:57.450
Don't you guys know the water's
cold in the deep sea?

524
00:24:57.450 --> 00:24:58.460
It's just like a bathtub...

525
00:24:58.460 --> 00:25:01.530
hot water rises and cold
sinks to the bottom.

526
00:25:01.530 --> 00:25:04.560
Oh, does it? I don't
take very many baths.

527
00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:07.870
Ah-ha! 'Cause heat rises and
there's not much sunlight,

528
00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:10.270
so there's nothing to heat
the water down here.

529
00:25:10.270 --> 00:25:14.070
The temperature's not the only
thing that's different at the bottom.

530
00:25:14.070 --> 00:25:16.310
You're not still
depressed, are you?!

531
00:25:16.310 --> 00:25:20.210
Once we move down, we'll find
something called deep currents.

532
00:25:17.670 --> 00:25:20.140
<b>Deep Current</b>

533
00:25:17.670 --> 00:25:17.870
<b>Surface
Current</b>
<b>Present
Location</b>

534
00:25:20.140 --> 00:25:23.170
<b>Deep Current</b>

535
00:25:20.210 --> 00:25:22.050
You don't run into
them terribly often,

536
00:25:21.500 --> 00:25:23.310
<b>Present
Location Surface
Current</b>

537
00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:21.640
<b>Surface
Current</b>

538
00:25:22.050 --> 00:25:25.050
but they're much larger
than the regular currents.

539
00:25:23.170 --> 00:25:23.270
<b>Deep Current</b>

540
00:25:23.170 --> 00:25:25.980
<b>Present
Location Surface
Current</b>

541
00:25:25.050 --> 00:25:27.220
Also, they're quite unique
in that their flow

542
00:25:25.640 --> 00:25:29.980
<b>Surface
Current</b>

543
00:25:25.880 --> 00:25:29.980
<b>Present
Location</b>

544
00:25:27.220 --> 00:25:28.790
is completely independent
of what you see

545
00:25:28.790 --> 00:25:29.980
closer to the surface.

546
00:25:30.250 --> 00:25:33.290
Currents under the currents and
you even have a diagram, huh?

547
00:25:33.290 --> 00:25:34.960
You sure do know a lot of stuff!

548
00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:38.230
That's because I'm so old!

549
00:25:38.230 --> 00:25:41.770
Not only that, but all currents
are connected to each other.

550
00:25:41.770 --> 00:25:45.000
But that doesn't mean they have
to flow in the same direction.

551
00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:48.410
They flow east,
west, up, and down.

552
00:25:48.410 --> 00:25:51.310
It's like a big, long
dragon, all tangled up.

553
00:25:51.310 --> 00:25:54.680
The deep currents tend to travel
at a surprisingly slow pace.

554
00:25:54.680 --> 00:25:56.280
After they reach the deep sea,

555
00:25:56.280 --> 00:25:59.850
it takes approximately 2,000 years
before they finally make their way

556
00:25:59.850 --> 00:26:01.820
up to the surface of the water.

557
00:26:01.820 --> 00:26:04.010
That makes me feel
young again, actually.

558
00:26:04.120 --> 00:26:05.990
– Two thousand?
– Two thousand?

559
00:26:05.990 --> 00:26:08.660
Yes, 2,000.

560
00:26:08.660 --> 00:26:11.300
And because of that,
there are countless tales

561
00:26:11.300 --> 00:26:15.130
about what lurks within
those dark deep currents.

562
00:26:15.130 --> 00:26:20.140
Monsters. Curses.
The wrath of the undead!

563
00:26:21.210 --> 00:26:23.170
You mean the deep
sea is haunted?

564
00:26:23.170 --> 00:26:25.480
What? The sea's haunted? Help!

565
00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:26.980
That sounds fun to me!

566
00:26:26.980 --> 00:26:28.850
Calm down, guys.

567
00:26:28.850 --> 00:26:31.350
I don't know anything about
the wrath of the undead,

568
00:26:31.350 --> 00:26:33.050
or whatever you're
freaking out about,

569
00:26:33.050 --> 00:26:36.150
but I do know that we'll encounter
a downward-flowing current soon.

570
00:26:36.150 --> 00:26:40.860
Once we do, we should be able
to ride it down into the deep sea.

571
00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:43.470
– She said something about
– She said something about

572
00:26:43.470 --> 00:26:45.800
– deep sea currents, I dunno.
– deep sea currents, I dunno.

573
00:26:45.110 --> 00:26:47.910
You morons could at least
pretend like you understand me!

574
00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:51.780
Shall we sing a duet, Nami?

575
00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:53.050
Shut up!

576
00:26:57.230 --> 00:26:58.970
It's here.

577
00:26:58.970 --> 00:27:00.740
Nami, I see it!

578
00:27:00.740 --> 00:27:01.900
Really?

579
00:27:01.900 --> 00:27:03.070
Let's check it out!

580
00:27:06.010 --> 00:27:08.610
That's freakin' sweet!

581
00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:10.310
– Whoa!
– Amazing!

582
00:27:10.310 --> 00:27:13.780
I admit, whatever that
thing is, it's pretty cool.

583
00:27:13.780 --> 00:27:17.250
This one's called
the Downward Plume.

584
00:27:18.590 --> 00:27:20.690
Cool!

585
00:27:20.690 --> 00:27:23.560
It's almost like
a giant waterfall.

586
00:27:25.700 --> 00:27:28.800
Except underwater!

587
00:27:29.530 --> 00:27:32.400
The sea is like falling
into the sea!

588
00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:35.210
Which doesn't make
sense, but I like it!

589
00:27:35.210 --> 00:27:37.180
It's going so fast!

590
00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:41.430
Mother Nature,
you're one crazy lady!

591
00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:43.430
What a spectacular sight.

592
00:27:43.430 --> 00:27:46.370
And from the looks of it, I'd
say the stream runs fairly deep.

593
00:27:46.370 --> 00:27:48.200
I've heard stories
about these currents,

594
00:27:48.200 --> 00:27:50.840
but they did not
do them justice!

595
00:27:50.840 --> 00:27:52.770
Ah! It's crazy dark down there!

596
00:27:52.770 --> 00:27:54.870
The floor's so far,
you can't even see it!

597
00:27:54.870 --> 00:27:56.610
We're dead!

598
00:27:56.610 --> 00:27:58.140
At this speed, we're
gonna get pile-driven

599
00:27:58.140 --> 00:27:59.850
straight down to the bottom!

600
00:28:02.010 --> 00:28:05.180
You sure it's safe
if we just go with the flow?

601
00:28:05.180 --> 00:28:07.390
If you're worried
about the ship, don't be.

602
00:28:07.390 --> 00:28:09.960
Sunny was built
from the treasure tree Adam.

603
00:28:09.960 --> 00:28:12.010
It's as tough as they come!

604
00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:16.160
Uh? Is that...?

605
00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:19.330
Crap. So it really
does live down here!

606
00:28:19.330 --> 00:28:21.240
It's no legend.

607
00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:24.610
A demon that
rules over the deep seas.

608
00:28:24.610 --> 00:28:28.990
The enemy of all mankind.

609
00:28:28.990 --> 00:28:31.190
Please, listen to me, Straw Hats!

610
00:28:31.190 --> 00:28:34.730
We have to turn back!
We're gonna die!

611
00:28:34.730 --> 00:28:36.490
Oh, yeah.
I forgot you were here.

612
00:28:36.490 --> 00:28:38.430
There's a sea monster!

613
00:28:39.130 --> 00:28:41.560
Uh... Usopp?

614
00:28:41.560 --> 00:28:43.630
Jeez, what is the matter
with you guys?

615
00:28:43.630 --> 00:28:45.900
Uh... look.

616
00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:48.140
Just spit it out.

617
00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:49.400
Oh?

618
00:28:50.810 --> 00:28:55.940
Uh-oh. It's here?

619
00:29:02.170 --> 00:29:04.940
It's the kraken!

620
00:29:06.110 --> 00:29:09.450
The legendary giant
octopus monster!

621
00:29:09.450 --> 00:29:12.320
He made fish
food outta those ships.

622
00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:15.690
They left for the New World
and never made it.

623
00:29:15.690 --> 00:29:20.130
Turn around! Please, I'm not
ready to die! I beg you!

624
00:29:20.130 --> 00:29:23.630
Just wait around for a couple
days and it'll probably go away!

625
00:29:23.630 --> 00:29:26.630
Hey, shut up for a second!

626
00:29:26.630 --> 00:29:28.670
I just had a perfect idea!

627
00:29:28.670 --> 00:29:32.710
Guys, we just have
to capture it!

628
00:29:32.710 --> 00:29:34.470
Huh?
